---
description: Patterns for win32com Excel automation, VBA component handling, .xlam file locks and UTF-8/ANSI encoding in this project
globs: ["**/*.py"]
alwaysApply: true
---

The authoritative rules for this project live in [.cursorrules](../../.cursorrules)
at the repository root. Read that file before changing any code under `xlvbesync/`.

Non-negotiables, summarised:

1. Only `xlvbesync/core/` may call `win32com.client`. Higher layers use its helpers.
2. Attach with `get_excel_for(path)`, never raw `GetActiveObject` - Excel can
   run multiple instances and the wrong one silently reports false success.
3. Loaded `.xlam` add-ins do not appear in `Workbooks` iteration but do resolve
   via `Workbooks("Name.xlam")`. Use `find_workbook()` / `lookup_workbook()`.
4. `AddIn.Installed` can read `True` while the add-in is not loaded. Toggle
   `False` then `True` (`force_install()`) to force a real load.
5. Iterate COM collections by index, never with a Python `for`.
6. Wrap mutations in `with quiet_excel(app):` so Excel's alert/event flags are
   always restored.
7. Retry only `-2147418111` and `-2147417846` via `@com_retry()`. Decode
   HRESULTs with `hresult_of()`, which unwraps `DISP_E_EXCEPTION`.
8. All disk files are UTF-8 (no BOM, CRLF); the VBE is ANSI. Always transcode
   through `xlvbesync/core/codec.py`. Report lossy characters, never silently
   write `?`.
9. Document modules (type 100) are line-synced in place and matched by sheet
   CodeName; they cannot be imported, created or removed.
10. For `.xlam` swaps: stage a copy, unload from every instance, poll for the
    handle to drop, `os.replace`, then reinstall inside a `finally`.
11. Never touch add-ins that are not configured project targets.
12. Use `xlvbesync.core.console`, never `print()`.
